.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt MKE2FS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mke2fs
.Nd make an ext2 file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl FnqSvV
.Op Fl b Ar block-size
.Op Fl d Ar root-directory
.Op Fl E Ar extended-options
.Op Fl g Ar blocks-per-group
.Op Fl i Ar bytes-per-inode
.Op Fl I Ar inode-size
.Op Fl j
.Op Fl L Ar volume-label
.Op Fl m Ar reserved-percentage
.Op Fl M Ar last-mounted-directory
.Op Fl N Ar number-of-inodes
.Op Fl O Oo ^ Oc Ns Ar feature Ns Op , Ns Ar ...
.Op Fl r Ar revision
.Op Fl t Ar fs-type
.Op Fl T Ar usage-type
.Op Fl U Ar UUID
.Op Fl z Ar undo-file
.Ar device
.Op Ar size
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes a new ext2 file system on
.Ar device ,
a partition node such as
.Pa /dev/disk0p4
or a regular file.
A
.Ar size
of blocks, or of bytes with a suffix of
.Ql k ,
.Ql m ,
.Ql g
or
.Ql t ,
limits the volume; for a regular file it is required when the file is
empty, and the file is extended to it.
.Pp
The ordinary kernel writes no disk node, so on the installed system and
on os-real
.Nm
makes a volume in a file, which
.Xr loopctl 8
attaches and
.Xr mount 8
mounts.
The recovery kernel's partition nodes take a volume directly.
.Pp
Rach2 uses e2fsprogs 1.47.4, linked statically against the ported musl,
and installs
.Nm
at
.Pa /sbin/mke2fs
on os-real, the GNU system and the recovery image.
Its defaults are compiled in from
.Pa vendor/e2fsprogs/mke2fs.conf
and are what the kernel's ext2 driver mounts writable: revision 1, 4 KiB
blocks, 256-byte inodes, an inode for every 16 KiB, and the features
.Ql sparse_super ,
.Ql large_file ,
.Ql filetype
and
.Ql resize_inode .
Upstream's defaults add
.Ql dir_index ,
which the kernel mounts read-only, and
.Ql ext_attr .
The types
.Ql small
and
.Ql floppy ,
which upstream gives 1 KiB blocks, keep 4 KiB blocks here for the same
reason.
.Pp
The kernel mounts read-only a volume with a block size below 4096,
.Ql dir_index
or a read-only-compatible feature it does not know, and refuses one with
an incompatible feature other than
.Ql filetype .
So
.Fl t Cm ext4
makes a volume this kernel does not mount, and
.Fl j
or
.Fl t Cm ext3
one whose journal it neither replays nor writes.
.Pp
The kernel has no discard request and no
.Pa /sys/fs/ext4 ,
so nothing is discarded and every inode table is written with zeros
when the volume is made.
.Nm
reads
.Pa /proc/mounts
to refuse a mounted device.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b Ar block-size
The block size in bytes: 1024, 2048 or 4096.
.It Fl d Ar root-directory
Copy the tree under
.Ar root-directory
into the new volume's root.
A tar archive in its place is refused, since libarchive is not built.
.It Fl E Ar extended-options
Comma-separated options, among them
.Ql root_owner Ns Op = Ns Ar uid : Ns Ar gid ,
.Ql stride= Ns Ar blocks ,
.Ql stripe_width= Ns Ar blocks ,
.Ql resize= Ns Ar max-blocks ,
.Ql nodiscard
and
.Ql test_fs .
.It Fl F
Make the volume even on a device that seems unsuitable or mounted.
.It Fl g Ar blocks-per-group
The blocks in a group, a multiple of 8.
.It Fl i Ar bytes-per-inode
One inode for every
.Ar bytes-per-inode
bytes of the volume.
.It Fl I Ar inode-size
The inode size, 128 or 256; the kernel takes no other.
.It Fl j
Add a journal: an ext3 volume.
.It Fl L Ar volume-label
The label, at most 16 bytes.
.It Fl m Ar reserved-percentage
The share of blocks only root may take, 5 by default.
.It Fl M Ar last-mounted-directory
The directory recorded as where the volume was last mounted.
.It Fl n
Show what would be made, and write nothing.
.It Fl N Ar number-of-inodes
The number of inodes, which overrides
.Fl i .
.It Fl O Ar features
Features to add, or with a leading
.Ql ^
to clear, after the defaults.
.It Fl q
Print nothing but errors.
.It Fl r Ar revision
0 for the original format, which has no features and 128-byte inodes, or
1.
.It Fl S
Write the superblock and the group descriptors only, for a volume whose
superblocks are lost; then run
.Xr e2fsck 8 .
.It Fl t Ar fs-type
.Cm ext2 ,
.Cm ext3
or
.Cm ext4 .
.It Fl T Ar usage-type
.Cm small ,
.Cm floppy ,
.Cm big ,
.Cm huge ,
.Cm news ,
.Cm largefile
or
.Cm largefile4 ,
which change the inode ratio.
Without it the type follows the size: under 3 MiB
.Cm floppy ,
under 512 MiB
.Cm small .
.It Fl U Ar UUID
The volume's UUID, or
.Cm clear ,
.Cm random
or
.Cm time .
.It Fl v
Print more.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl z Ar undo-file
Save each block's old contents in
.Ar undo-file
before it is overwritten.
.El
.Pp
.Fl c
and
.Fl l
need
.Ic badblocks ,
which is not installed.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/mke2fs.conf
.It Pa /sbin/mke2fs
The program.
.It Pa /etc/mke2fs.conf
The configuration, read in place of the compiled-in defaults when it
exists; no image stages one.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the volume is made, 1 otherwise.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Make an 8 MiB volume in a file, then mount it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mke2fs -q /root/v.ext2 8M
loopctl attach 0 /root/v.ext2
mount -t ext2 /dev/loop0 /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
On the recovery system, make a volume labelled
.Ql data
on the fourth partition:
.Dl mke2fs -L data /dev/disk0p4
.Pp
In the source tree,
.Ql tests/run ext2
makes both and checks them.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr e2fsck 8 ,
.Xr loopctl 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr resize2fs 8
.Sh HISTORY
